===Etymology 3===
{{coin|en|John Tukey|in=1946|nobycat=1}} as an abbreviation of {{m|en|binary digit}}, probably influenced by connotations of “small portion”.<ref>{{cite-web|title=Six Receive Honorary Degrees at Princeton Commencement|url=http://www.princeton.edu/pr/news/98/q2/0602-honorary.html|archivedate=2002-02-09|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20020209025010/http://www.princeton.edu/pr/news/98/q2/0602-honorary.html}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web |url=http://www.thocp.net/timeline/1944.htm#1946 |accessdate=23 March 2007 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20070303171906/http://www.thocp.net/timeline/1944.htm#1946 |archivedate=3 March 2007 }}</ref> First used in print 1948 by {{w|Claude Shannon}}.<ref>{{cite-journal
|author=Claude Shannon
|authorlink=Claude Shannon
|year=1948
|month=July
|work=The Bell System Technical Journal
|doi=10.1002/j.1538-7305.1948.tb01338.x
|title=A Mathematical Theory of Communication}}
</ref> Compare {{m|en|byte}} and {{m|en|nybble}}, with similar food associations.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|binary digit}} {{lb|en|mathematics|computing}} A [[binary digit]], generally represented as a 1 or 0.
# {{lb|en|computing}} The smallest unit of storage in a [[digital computer]], consisting of a binary digit.
#: {{syn|en|b}}
# {{senseid|en|datum}} {{lb|en|information theory|cryptography}} Any [[datum]] that may take on one of exactly two values.
#: {{ux|en|status '''bits''' on IRC}}
#: {{ux|en|permission '''bits''' in a file system}}
# {{lb|en|information theory}} A [[unit of measure]] for [[information entropy]].
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2011-05-17
|author=Lisa Grossman
|title=Entropy Is Universal Rule of Language
|site=Wired Science
|url=http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2011/05/universal-entropy/
|accessdate=2012-09-26
|passage=The researchers found that the original texts spanned a variety of entropy values in different languages, reflecting differences in grammar and structure.<br>But strangely, the difference in entropy between the original, ordered text and the randomly scrambled text was constant across languages. This difference is a way to measure the amount of information encoded in word order, Montemurro says. The amount of information lost when they scrambled the text was about 3.5 '''bits''' per word.}}
# A {{l|en|microbitcoin}}, or a millionth of a {{l|en|bitcoin}} (0.000001 BTC).

